## Spectral Deferred Correction

`SDC` from OrdinaryDiffEqSDC iterates a cheap sweeper over the nodes of a
collocation problem instead of applying a fixed tableau. With the explicit-Euler
sweeper `SDCSweeper.FE`, each node update is one explicit Euler step between
adjacent nodes plus a correction integral. Every attempted step first evaluates
the right-hand side at all `M` nodes during copy initialisation and then once
per node in each of the `K` sweeps, so a step on `M` nodes costs `(K+1)*M`
evaluations: 20 for `num_sweeps = 4` and 32 for `num_sweeps = 7` on the four
nodes used here. Each sweep raises the order by about one until the collocation
order is reached, so `num_sweeps` rather than a new tableau is what changes the
accuracy. The default `SDCStepUpdate.Quadrature` step update adds one further
order, so on the default Radau-right Legendre nodes `num_nodes = 4` —
collocation order 7 — `num_sweeps = 4` gives an adaptive method of solution
order 5 and `num_sweeps = 7` reaches the collocation cap of 7. The error
estimate is the difference between the last two sweep updates, so the embedded
solution is the step update formed one sweep earlier: the 4-sweep pair is
orders 5 and 4. At `num_sweeps = 7` the embedded update is the six-sweep
value, which already reaches the same order-7 cap, so both updates are order
7 — the adaptive-order value of 6 that OrdinaryDiffEqSDC reports is the
[reported order rule](https://github.com/SciML/OrdinaryDiffEq.jl/blob/OrdinaryDiffEqSDC-v1.0.0/lib/OrdinaryDiffEqSDC/src/alg_utils.jl#L16-L35)
(solution order minus one), not the achieved order of the
[embedded update](https://github.com/SciML/OrdinaryDiffEq.jl/blob/OrdinaryDiffEqSDC-v1.0.0/lib/OrdinaryDiffEqSDC/src/sdc_perform_step.jl#L271-L287).

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>Tsit5(), :tags=>PLEIADES_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>Vern9(), :tags=>PLEIADES_RK_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>SDC(sweeper = SDCSweeper.FE, num_nodes = 4, num_sweeps = 4), :tags=>PLEIADES_SDC_TAGS)
          Dict(:alg=>SDC(sweeper = SDCSweeper.FE, num_nodes = 4, num_sweeps = 7), :tags=>PLEIADES_SDC_TAGS)]
solnames = ["Tsit5", "Vern9", "SDC-FE (4 nodes, 4 sweeps)", "SDC-FE (4 nodes, 7 sweeps)"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; appxsol = test_sol, error_estimates = [:final], names = solnames,
    save_everystep = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 100)
print_work_table(wp, PLEIADES_ERROR_FLOOR)
plot(wp)
```

How many right-hand-side evaluations does each sweep count spend, and are any of
the SDC points only reporting the accuracy of the reference trajectory? The
reference is bounded against a tighter solve first; an achieved error within an
order of magnitude of that gap is marked reference-limited.

```julia
using Statistics
tighter_ref = solve(prob, Vern9(), abstol = 1 / 10^14, reltol = 1 / 10^12,
    maxiters = 1000000, save_everystep = false, dense = false)
ref_floor = mean(abs.(sol.u[end] - tighter_ref.u[end]))
println("Final-point gap between the reference and a tighter solve: ", ref_floor)
for (name, setup) in zip(solnames[3:4], setups[3:4])
    for (abstol, reltol) in zip(abstols, reltols)
        s = solve(prob, setup[:alg]; abstol = abstol, reltol = reltol)
        err = mean(abs.(s.u[end] - test_sol(s.t[end])))
        flag = err <= 10 * ref_floor ? " (reference-limited)" : ""
        println("$name abstol=$abstol reltol=$reltol: nf=$(s.stats.nf), final error=$err$flag")
    end
end
```

The 7-sweep method takes far fewer steps at each tolerance, but each step costs
about 1.6× more evaluations, so the two curves nearly coincide on the
error-against-work plane: around a 2e-3–3e-3 final error both sweep counts
spend roughly 2400 evaluations and about the same time, which is the one place
the extra sweeps recover their per-step cost. Elsewhere they do not. At the
loosest tolerance both spend about 1700 evaluations yet the 4-sweep run is
about twenty times more accurate, and at the tight end the 7-sweep runs never
get below a 1e-3 final error while the 4-sweep method continues to about 2e-5.
Those are endpoint errors; the audit records no per-step local estimates, so
this page cannot say how the sweep-difference estimate is calibrated against
the [tolerances the step-size controller
targets](https://docs.sciml.ai/DiffEqDocs/stable/basics/common_solver_opts/#Basic-Stepsize-Control). Neither SDC
variant is reference-limited at any tolerance: the reference trajectory agrees
with a tighter solve to about 5e-10 in the same mean-absolute final error, more
than four orders of magnitude below the smallest error either reaches here. Both explicit SDC variants sit above the Tsit5 and Vern9 lines at
every accuracy level — the repeated first-order sweeps spend more evaluations
per unit of accuracy than a purpose-built high-order Runge–Kutta step on this
smooth non-stiff problem.


```julia; echo=false; results="hidden"; error=false
let
    for w in low_order_wp.wps, metric in propertynames(w.errors)
        metric === :final || @assert !any(isfinite, getproperty(w.errors, metric)) "low-order $(w.name) stores a finite $metric error"
    end
    @assert length(sol_endpoint.t) == 2
    @assert !sol_endpoint.dense
    @assert length(tighter_ref.t) == 2
    @assert !tighter_ref.dense
    dense_wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, low_order_setups; appxsol = test_sol_dense,
        error_estimates = [:final], save_everystep = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 1, maxiters = 10000)
    for (w, d) in zip(low_order_wp.wps, dense_wp.wps)
        @assert isequal(w.errors.final, d.errors.final) "low-order $(w.name) endpoint errors differ"
        for (a, b) in zip(w.stats, d.stats), field in fieldnames(typeof(a))
            @assert isequal(getfield(a, field), getfield(b, field)) "low-order $(w.name) $field differs"
        end
    end
end
```

```julia; echo=false; results="hidden"; error=false
assert_work_table_markdown_fixture()
```

## Conclusion

The OrdinaryDiffEq.jl algorithms come out as faster in each case than the ODEInterface algorithms. Overall, the Verner methods have a really good showing once again. The `CVODE_Adams` method does really well here when the tolerances are higher.

```julia, echo = false, tangle = false
using SciMLBenchmarks
SciMLBenchmarks.bench_footer(WEAVE_ARGS[:folder], WEAVE_ARGS[:file])
```
